{
  "nbformat": 4,
  "nbformat_minor": 5,
  "metadata": {
    "kernelspec": {
      "name": "maxima",
      "display_name": "Maxima",
      "language": "maxima"
    },
    "aximar": {}
  },
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Digital Filter Design\n\nThis notebook designs a digital low-pass filter from first principles. We derive the analog Butterworth prototype symbolically, map it to a digital filter with the bilinear transform, and read off the coefficients of the difference equation. The filter then runs on a noisy signal, and an FFT checks the attenuation it achieves against the attenuation the design predicts.\n\nA Chebyshev design at the end shows the trade between a flat passband and a steep transition.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"orthopoly\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## The Butterworth Prototype [S]\n\nAn order-$n$ Butterworth low-pass filter has the flattest possible passband. Its squared magnitude response, with the cutoff normalised to $\\omega_c = 1$, is\n\n$$|H(j\\omega)|^2 = \\frac{1}{1 + \\omega^{2n}}$$\n\nIts poles sit evenly spaced on the unit circle in the left half of the $s$-plane:\n\n$$s_k = e^{\\,i\\pi(2k + n - 1)/(2n)}, \\quad k = 1, \\dots, n$$\n\nMultiplying out $\\prod_k (s - s_k)$ gives the denominator polynomial. The complex poles come in conjugate pairs, so the coefficients are real.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "butter_poles(n) := makelist(exp(%i*%pi*(2*k + n - 1)/(2*n)), k, 1, n)$\nbutter_den(n) := expand(realpart(rectform(\n  expand(apply(\"*\", map(lambda([p], s - p), butter_poles(n)))))))$\n\nfor n : 1 thru 3 do print(\"n =\", n, \":  \", butter_den(n))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 2,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "n = 1 :   s+1\nn = 2 :   s^2+sqrt(2)*s+1\nn = 3 :   s^3+2*s^2+2*s+1"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "From $n = 4$ the exact coefficients involve $\\cos(\\pi/8)$ and friends, so we show them as decimals:",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "expand(float(butter_den(4)));",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 3,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "s^4+2.613125929752753\\,s^3+3.414213562373095\\,s^2+2.613125929752753\\,s+1.0"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 3,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "For $n = 2$ the denominator is $s^2 + \\sqrt{2}\\,s + 1$. Substituting $s = j\\omega$ and taking the squared modulus recovers the Butterworth response exactly:",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "H2 : 1 / butter_den(2)$\nratsimp(cabs(subst(s = %i*w, H2))^2);",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "{{1}\\over{w^4+1}}"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 4,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Frequency Response and Order [S+N]\n\nIn decibels the magnitude is $-10\\log_{10}(1 + \\omega^{2n})$. Every order passes through $-3$ dB at the cutoff, and above it the response falls by $20n$ dB per decade. A higher order buys a sharper cut-off at the cost of more poles to implement.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "w_list : np_to_list(np_logspace(-1, 1.5, 300))$\nmag_db(n) := map(lambda([w], -10*log(1 + w^(2*n))/log(10.0)), w_list)$\n\nax_draw2d(\n  name=\"n = 1\", lines(w_list, mag_db(1)),\n  name=\"n = 2\", lines(w_list, mag_db(2)),\n  name=\"n = 4\", lines(w_list, mag_db(4)),\n  name=\"n = 6\", lines(w_list, mag_db(6)),\n  color=\"gray\", dash=\"dash\", name=\"-3 dB\", lines([0.1, 31.6], [-3, -3]),\n  logx=true, yrange=[-80, 5],\n  title=\"Butterworth Magnitude Response\",\n  xlabel=\"Normalised frequency ω/ωc\", ylabel=\"Magnitude (dB)\",\n  grid=true, showlegend=true\n)$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 5,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "application/x-maxima-plotly": [
